{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1995 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c5230c27cd34c9b2c025dddf4e289cc3805da5d2aa17bddf32e7d03cdde018bf" :edb/db-before-t 1994 :edb/db-after-t 1995 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535307 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:44:23.209Z" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343034 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343039 13194139535307 false] [17592186343034 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343051 13194139535307 true] [17592186343034 1100 "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139535307 false] [17592186343034 1100 "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nWork over the reals. Use the classification \"outside the real coefficient domain\" at points separated from the coefficient domain. At a boundary of the coefficient domain, classify singularity by whether the normalized coefficients, or their required weighted forms, admit analytic extension from that domain." 13194139535307 true] [17592186343034 1145 "For the original real logarithm $\\ln x$, the coefficient domain is $x>0$. Normalize the equation on that domain away from its zero leading coefficient:\n\n$$y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad P(x)=\\frac1{x(x+1)},\\quad Q(x)=\\frac{\\ln x}{x-9}.$$\n\nAt $x=3$, both normalized coefficients are analytic, so the point is ordinary.\n\nThe point $x=-1$ lies outside the real coefficient domain. There is no real neighborhood of $-1$ on which $\\ln x$ is defined, and it is not a boundary point approached from $x>0$. Therefore the correct classification is \"outside the real coefficient domain\". Calling it a real regular or irregular singular point would presuppose a local differential equation that is not defined there.\n\nThe point $x=0$ is a singular boundary point. From $x>0$, $xP=1/(x+1)$ has an analytic extension, but $x^2Q=x^2\\ln x/(x-9)$ does not: its second derivative has leading divergent term $-(2/9)\\ln x$. Therefore zero is irregular singular under the stated analytic extension criterion. A finite weighted-coefficient limit alone does not establish analyticity.\n\nAt $x=9$, $Q$ has a pole, while $(x-9)P$ and $(x-9)^2Q=(x-9)\\ln x$ are analytic. Therefore $x=9$ is regular singular.\n\nReplacing the original coefficient by $\\ln|x|$ would define a different equation and make $-1$ regular singular." 13194139535307 true] [17592186343034 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-9)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-9=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=9$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=9.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1,$ so is *not* analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=9,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)P(x)=(x-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=9,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)^{2}Q(x)=(x-9)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}=(x-9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=9.$ Therefore, $x=9$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139535307 false] [17592186343051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343052 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343053 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343054 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343055 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1041 "field-2" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343055 13194139535307 true] [17592186343051 1110 #uuid "c586ee04-a0c2-506c-8bc2-428a175dab8c" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343052 1034 "an ordinary" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343052 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535307 true] [17592186343052 1111 #uuid "3714ef30-6f75-5fa4-988a-c62c4c89d1b3" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343053 1034 "a regular singular" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535307 true] [17592186343053 1111 #uuid "15575576-7219-5852-bb31-1ae68bfbb0c9" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343054 1034 "an irregular singular" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343054 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535307 true] [17592186343054 1111 #uuid "510ee0da-7a3b-5ea9-b9df-6d0fbee571af" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343055 1034 "outside the real coefficient domain" 13194139535307 true] [17592186343055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535307 true] [17592186343055 1111 #uuid "8d757184-c59c-59ff-9d73-ebdd4e1e4370" 13194139535307 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2" 17592186343051 "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-0" 17592186343052 "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-1" 17592186343053 "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-2" 17592186343054 "q-342644/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-3" 17592186343055}}
